From d7fc36115cd0148a6b906fcd8d3f0673c2ea822d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Noel Blatter <noel.blatter@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 21:17:19 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Build step: shared constant index arrays, cheaper sideband
 layout

run_build_step creates several thousand tiny index arrays per setup, and
creating one costs more than everything that happens to it afterwards.

- Output selections and empty function inputs that are the same for every
  component of a kind are shared read-only arrays (never positions in a
  system matrix, which the sideband layout shifts in place).
- Function inputs come with their seven columns from the call sites, so
  set_instructions no longer pads them.
- One signal per space, the usual case, needs no np.tile and np.repeat.
- The sideband layout has a short path for instruction sets that lie
  entirely in the carrier block (surfaces, noise sources).
- np.array instead of np.full for the constant output rows.

All arrays of run_build_step are identical for 27 setups (946 arrays,
runs/noel/test_build.py --dump / --compare), the benchmark arrays are
bit-identical, all scripts under runs/philip and runs/flags pass.

Apple M4: run_build_step 3.97 -> 3.38 ms warm, 4.66 -> 4.12 ms on the
first call. Build phase of benchmark.py, 14 alternating runs, median:
10.9 -> 10.5 ms (macOS), 9.9 -> 9.3 ms (Docker, Linux aarch64, one CPU).
Not measured on the Xeon. Shipped bytecode of build.py regenerated.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 .../__pycache__/build.cpython-312.pyc         | Bin 60331 -> 62580 bytes
 differometor/build.py                         | 144 ++++++++++++------
 2 files changed, 98 insertions(+), 46 deletions(-)

